{ Next trick: the decoy. Drag his king to a square where a fork is waiting. Take on e eight. [%cal Ye1e8][%csl Rc7] } 17... Re8 18. Rxe8+ { He must take with the king, and that square is not a safe one. [%csl Rd8] } (18. Red1 { You keep the rook and the whole idea is gone. } 18... Re6 19. Rd2 Rc8 20. Rad1 Rd6 { A dull equal game, and you were winning. }) (18. c4 { A quiet pawn move while a fork was waiting. } 18... Rxe1+ 19. Rxe1 Bd6 20. b4 b6 { Nothing left here. }) 18... Kxe8 { Now his king and his bishop stand on two forking squares. [%csl Re8,Rc7] } 19. Nxc7+ { Check, and a free bishop. That is a decoy: bring him where you want him. } (19. Re1+ { A check, but the fork was the whole point. } 19... Kd8 20. Re7 Rc8 21. Rxf7 Be5 { A pawn instead of a piece. }) (19. Rd1 { A calm move, and the fork disappears. } 19... Bd8 20. c3 Nb6 21. Nxb6 axb6 { Level again. }) 1-0


